From 90e63b584ec61839c9a683173aeecd1103c7966a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 08:40:25 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 137/144] video: decode video textures with FFmpeg instead of
 libmpv

Every video texture (and video wallpaper) ran a libmpv core with a render context of its own: mpv's CUDA interop made a
CUDA context per player unless libmpv carried an option no release has (0136), mpv played on its own wall clock rather
than the scene's, and its renderer's textures and passes held device memory a texture needs none of.

GLPlayer is now FFmpeg (libavformat, libavcodec, libavutil; libswscale for odd formats) and one draw:
- VideoDecoder demuxes the texture's bytes (or a file) and decodes on a thread of its own ("wp-video"), a few frames
  ahead of the player's clock; every frame carries its time on that clock, which runs on across loops, so a loop's
  frames follow the last one's at the video's length (gapless). A frame already due replaces the due one before it.
- hardware, per wp_context_set_video_hwdec (still mpv's names): `nvdec`/`cuda`, `*-copy`, `vaapi`, `vulkan`, `vdpau`,
  `auto`, `auto-copy`, `no`, tried in order and only where a decoder of the codec can use them (FFmpeg's own `av1`
  when libdav1d is preferred); software when the list runs
  out or the hardware refuses a stream (FFmpeg's get_format falls back on the same decoder). Each device type opens
  once per process and every video shares it: CUDA on the GPU's primary context (FFmpeg's `primary_ctx`), released
  with its last video.
- with the `cuda` interop (or none named) a CUDA frame's planes are copied into GL textures registered with
  cuGraphicsGLRegisterImage, on the GPU (libcuda looked up by soname, as FFmpeg loaded it); every other hardware frame
  is copied to memory and uploaded. One shader converts the planes (NV12, P010, planar 4:2:0/4:2:2/4:4:4 at 8-16 bits,
  with alpha when there is one) to RGBA by the frame's matrix and range (mpv's guesses when unspecified: BT.709 from
  720p up, limited range, left-sited chroma); anything else goes through swscale. The GL state the draw touches is
  put back.
- the video plays on the scene's clock: the context's render time, so the host's speed and pauses; IVideoTexture's
  play, pause, stop, seek, rate, loop and ended keep their meaning. A decoder that falls a second behind (a rate it
  can't keep up with) jumps to the clock.
- a scene video texture starts decoding when the build creates it, and a player's first render waits up to 500 ms for
  its first frame: the scene's first picture shows its videos.
- nothing is muted any more because no audio is decoded: a video wallpaper (CVideo) plays silent too.
libmpv is no longer linked by the core.

Measured by liascape's scene_load.py (private headless player, RTX 3070 Ti), 3426575339 (two 1080p H.264 video
textures) against 162fbd2 with liascape's patched libmpv (medians, base / this):
- worker VRAM 742 / 446 MiB (mpv's renderer and its per-player surfaces gone; one CUDA context as before)
- source.ready warm 293 / 291 ms, cold 392 / 315, boot 312 / 331 (n 18 / 2 / 2); both videos are in that first
  frame, where mpv's came 96-150 ms after it: the complete picture warm 406 / 291, cold 489 / 315, boot 462 / 331
- black loads 0 / 0; 3042492564 (no video) warm 465 / 463 ms, 1159 / 1159 MiB
- the CUDA device (the primary context) takes ~90 ms of the ~130 ms a video needs to its first frame
The library's other video textures (H.264 at 2340x1080, 4000x2800 and 3840x2160) and the first of them re-encoded as
HEVC 8 and 10 bit, VP9 and AV1 (all on NVDEC through the interop) and H.264 4:4:4 (software, NVDEC has no 4:4:4 H.264)
render like mpv's frames at the same scene times (mean |diff| 2-11 of 255, from the animation's phase), where mpv still
showed black at 0.5 s in two of them.

Tests (liascape's host_api_test): video_players_load_only_the_hosts_interop (the devices each hwdec list opens, CUDA
frames through the interop or copied, the video's red on the layer), video_players_share_one_cuda_context (two videos
open one CUDA device on the primary context, the second adds < 64 MiB; red with the sharing taken out: two devices,
+169 MiB), video_texture_plays_on_the_scene_clock (a four-colour clip's frames follow the host's time, loop without a
gap, hold while the host does), video_texture_api.

Origin: ours
Why: liascape docs/TODO.md #104 (scene video textures: one CUDA context per scene, on every install)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
